.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt QGA-GUEST-EXEC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm qga-guest-exec
.Nd execute commands in a guest via the guest agent protocol
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl w Ar wait
.Ar socket
.Ar program
.Op Ar args ...
.Nm
.Op Fl q
.Fl a
.Ar socket
.Ar program
.Op Ar args ...
.Nm
.Op Fl w Ar wait
.Fl p Ar pid
.Ar socket
.Nm
.Fl v
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility connects to the guest agent socket at
.Ar socket
and asks the agent to execute
.Ar program
inside the guest.
.Pp
The guest agent is the software running inside the virtual machine
that implements the QEMU Guest Agent Protocol.
The protocol is defined by QEMU, but the agent itself is independent
of the virtual machine monitor: the same
.Dq guest-exec
and
.Dq guest-exec-status
commands are available whenever the protocol is spoken.
The virtual machine monitor only provides the transport, by exposing
a virtio-serial or virtio-console port as a Unix domain socket on the
host.
Both
.Xr bhyve 8
and QEMU/KVM can do this, and so can any other VMM that forwards the
port.
.Pp
The agent returns a process identifier immediately; the
.Dq guest-exec
operation is asynchronous by design.
.Nm
then polls the
.Dq guest-exec-status
command until the process exits or the timeout is reached.
If output capture is enabled (the default), the standard output and
standard error produced by the guest process are decoded from base64
and written to the corresponding streams of
.Nm .
.Pp
The
.Ar socket
argument is mandatory and must be a path to a Unix domain socket
created by the virtual machine monitor for the virtio-console port
that carries the guest agent.
.Nm
verifies that the path exists and is a socket before contacting the
agent.
.Pp
Before each command,
.Nm
issues a
.Dq guest-sync-delimited
request with a random identifier and verifies the response.
This protects against stale or interleaved replies on the socket.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Launch
.Ar program
in the guest and print the guest process identifier on standard output.
Exit immediately after the identifier is printed.
This option cannot be combined with
.Fl p .
.It Fl q
Disable output capture in the guest.
The agent will not collect standard output or standard error, and
.Nm
will produce no guest output.
When used together with
.Fl a ,
this determines whether a later
.Fl p
invocation can retrieve output for the process.
When used together with
.Fl p ,
this option has no effect, because capture was decided when the process
was originally launched.
.It Fl p Ar pid
Resume polling an existing guest process previously launched with
.Fl a .
No new command is started, and
.Ar program
must not be given.
The value
.Ar pid
must be a positive decimal integer.
This option cannot be combined with
.Fl a .
.It Fl w Ar wait
Set the maximum number of seconds to wait for the guest process to
exit.
The default is 30 seconds.
A value of 0 disables the deadline, causing
.Nm
to wait indefinitely.
The maximum accepted value is 86400 (one day).
This option has no effect when used together with
.Fl a ,
because
.Nm
returns before entering the polling loop.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The guest process exited with status 0, or
.Fl a
was used and the identifier was printed successfully.
.It 1
Usage error, invalid option argument, an internal failure
(such as a malformed response that
.Xr jq 1
could not parse), or
.Ar socket
does not exist or is not a Unix domain socket.
.It 2
No sync delimiter was received from the guest agent.
This usually indicates that the socket is not connected to a live
agent.
.It 3
The guest agent response did not consist of the expected two lines.
.It 4
The guest agent returned an error.
The error description is printed on standard error.
.It 5
The
.Dq guest-sync
identifier in the response did not match the identifier that was sent.
.It 6
The timeout specified with
.Fl w
expired before the guest process exited.
.It 127
The guest process exited with a non-zero status.
The exact status is not propagated.
.It 128
The guest process was terminated by a signal, or the exit status could
not be determined.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jq 1 ,
.Xr nc 1 ,
.Xr bhyve 8 ,
.Xr qemu-ga-ref 7
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Jesús Daniel Colmenares Oviedo Aq Mt DtxdF@disroot.org
.Sh CAVEATS
The
.Dq merged
value for the
.Va capture-output
argument of
.Dq guest-exec
is not supported by Windows guests.
Consequently,
.Nm
always requests separate stdout and stderr capture and writes each
stream to the corresponding local stream.
The relative ordering of lines from the two streams is therefore
not preserved.
.Pp
The socket used by the guest agent may remain open even when no agent
is listening.
.Nm
relies on a short connection timeout for each request and on the
.Dq guest-sync-delimited
handshake to detect a dead or unresponsive agent.
.Pp
The exit status of the guest process is collapsed to 127 for any
non-zero value.
Scripts that need the exact status should query
.Dq guest-exec-status
directly.
.Sh BUGS
With
.Fl w Ar 0
there is no upper bound on the number of status polls.
If the agent returns a response without the
.Va exited
field, or with
.Va exited
set to
.Dv null ,
.Nm
will loop indefinitely.
Use a non-zero timeout to avoid this situation.
